# §21 step 5 — vault-dashboard scheduled wake (app/wake.py).
#
# Install (once per machine, needs your password once — nothing automates this):
#     sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g wheel \
#       backend/scripts/kestrel-wake.sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/kestrel-wake
# Verify:  sudo -n -l | grep pmset
# Remove:  sudo rm /etc/sudoers.d/kestrel-wake
#
# Not installed by anything in the repo, and NOT carried over by Migration
# Assistant — a new Mac starts without it. Nothing breaks loudly if it is
# missing: the two write verbs fail on `sudo -n`, apply() logs and retries, and
# the wake half degrades to exactly the pre-step-5 behaviour. Alarms are already
# on the phone via iCloud and fire regardless; what is lost is the overnight
# reconcile that keeps a *moved* flight's alarm honest.
#
# The `tslom` below is a username, not a variable. Change it if the account
# changes, then re-check with `visudo -c -f` before installing.
#
# `pmset schedule` checks euid and refuses for an admin user; the rights
# database's system.preferences.energysaver entry governs the Energy Saver
# pane through the Admin framework and never reaches this binary.
#
# Scoped to the two calls app/wake.py makes — `schedule wake <date> <owner>`
# and `schedule cancel wake <date> <owner>` — so a process running as you
# can't use it to schedule a shutdown/sleep/restart or `cancelall` other
# owners' events. The SETTING verbs (`pmset -a`, `-b`, `-c`) stay out of reach.
# The absolute path matters — a bare `pmset` would match anything by that name
# earlier on $PATH.
tslom ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/pmset schedule wake *, /usr/bin/pmset schedule cancel wake *
